#heading(level: 2)[#text("What it is not")] <not>
#block(width: measure, above: 0pt, below: 0.9em)[#list([#strong[#text("Not a record format.")]#text(" It does not say what an auditable system should record, in what schema, or with what semantics. It defines the outer layer that carries someone else's records; the record grammar is the payload's own business, named and not specified here (§0.5 MA-5).")], [#strong[#text("Not a claim about the records.")]#text(" A conformant package may contain records that are false, and may omit records that existed. Conformance says the package is well-formed and self-consistent; it says nothing about truth or completeness (§2.2 MA-7).")], [#strong[#text("Not a certification, and not a registry.")]#text(" No registration, no permission and no fee is required. A third party claims conformance by publishing a statement in its own name, naming the requirements it relies on and any it does not satisfy, with the reason; the standard certifies no one (§2.4 MA-9).")], [#strong[#text("Not a service.")]#text(" The reference validator runs offline with a POSIX shell and the utilities §10.1 names: no network, no interpreter beyond those, no key material, no service, no producer (§10.1 MA-68).")], [#strong[#text("No keys, and no third party required for the base tier.")]#text(" A package with no third party, no seal, no timestamp and no key material is fully conformant; nothing in the conformance definition requires any of them (§9.1(a) and (b), MA-61). The anchor is an optional slot, not a tier.")], [#strong[#text("Not a ladder.")]#text(" There is one level, deliberately: a ladder reads as \"the lower tier is deficient\", which is backwards, since the unanchored tier is the adoption surface (§2.3 MA-8).")], [#strong[#text("Not a promise about the producer.")]#text(" A validator is not required to know a producer; ")#ic("producer")#text(" is a claim under the producer's own name (§10.8 MA-75).")], indent: 1.2em, spacing: 0.5em)]
#block(width: measure, above: 0pt, below: 0.9em)[#text("One implementation is named in the standard's Annex A, and only there. That annex is informative: nothing in it is required to conform, and removing it would leave the standard whole.")]
